  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="2112e782-b417-48b7-94a5-befe4d8b8ab6" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="c133d14f-dfe1-4d87-983d-a394d9d888d3" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(y=(x-2)^2 + 1\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=x^2 - 4\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x-4)^2 + 6\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=(x-2)^2 + 1\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=x^2 - 4\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=x(x+1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x+3)(x+3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x-4)^2 + 6\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> quadratic expressions written in vertex form.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(y=(x-2)^2 + 1, y=x^2 - 4, y=(x-4)^2 + 6\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.2 – 7.14.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="7b0b1508-7cef-415d-80fd-59c5381859fa" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Both expressions are equal to \(x^2+6x\)." data-solution-options="[&quot;Both expressions are equal to \\(x^2+6x\\).&quot;, &quot;Both expressions are equal to \\(x^2-6x\\).&quot;, &quot;Both expressions are equal to \\(2x+6\\).&quot;, &quot;Both expressions are equal to \\(x^2-3\\).&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Examine the following two equations and use them to answer questions 2 – 5. One defines function \(m\) and the other defines function \(p\).</para>
      <para> \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) </para>
        <para> \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\) </para>
      <para>How do you know that the expressions defining \(m\) and \(p\) are equivalent?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Both expressions are equal to \(x^2+6x\). \((x+3)^2−9=(x+3)(x+3)−9=x^2+3x+3x+9−9=x^2+6x\) and \(x(x+6)=x^2+6x\). </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.2 and 7.14.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="db7fd02c-7b6a-46cf-b8e0-55761c276bb8" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-3">
    <problem>
      <para>Recall \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\). What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(m\)? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -3.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="c98bb928-4560-4c4b-bbf4-ea0149ca6437" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-9">
    <problem>
      <para>Recall \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\). What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(m\)? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this function is -9.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="32b1b9fd-9c8e-42a7-819e-b7a0dd0c09da" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((0,0)\) and \((-6,0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((-6,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((6,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((0,-6)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((0,6)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Recall \(m(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(p(x)=(x+3)^2−9\). What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of \(p\)? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! (0,0) and (-6,0) The graphs of \(m\) and \(p\) are the same. The zeros of \(m\) are 0 and -6, which give the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.15.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--6a-->
